  "slug": "valley-magnetic-0172",
  "summary": "Five preprints show magnetic proximity, optically programmed order, and sliding ferroelectricity controlling valley and exciton responses in van der Waals stacks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is external control of excitonic and valley properties in van der Waals systems through adjacent ordered layers. WS2/CrSBr is reported to show type-II alignment, magnetic-proximity Zeeman valley splitting and spin-selective interlayer charge transfer, while WSe2/CrPS4 shows optical switching of valley polarization via a light-controlled Néel vector. Rhombohedral bilayer WSe2 exhibits hysteretic, electrically switchable exciton photoluminescence and Raman response through sliding ferroelectricity. Related papers extend the interlayer-coupling theme: spin-crossover molecules on monolayer TMDs couple via Förster energy transfer and charge transfer, and NbOCl2 flakes show room-temperature antiferroelectricity with reversible switching to a ferroelectric state, probed by SHG. New here is the range of switchable order parameters (magnetic, ferroelectric, optical) used as valley and exciton handles.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
